  <body>
    <div class="cover">
      <div class="company">SALE FISH MARKETING AND CONSULTING</div>
      <div class="tagline">OT Cybersecurity Practice</div>
      <div class="title">OT Cybersecurity<br/>Assessment Report</div>
      <p style="margin-top:30pt; font-size:13pt; font-weight:bold;">Riverside Production Facility</p>
      <p style="font-size:11pt;">Meridian Chemical Solutions</p>
      <p style="margin-top:20pt; font-size:12pt;">Chemical &nbsp;|&nbsp; IEC 62443</p>
      <p style="font-size:11pt; color:#595959;">November 21, 2026</p>
    </div>

    <div class="page-break"></div>
    <h1>Executive Summary</h1>
    <table class="meta-table">
      <tr><td class="meta-label">Site:</td><td>Riverside Production Facility</td><td class="meta-label">Organization:</td><td>Meridian Chemical Solutions</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="meta-label">Industry:</td><td>Chemical</td><td class="meta-label">Standard:</td><td>IEC 62443</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="meta-label">Assessment Mode:</td><td>Detailed</td><td class="meta-label">Date:</td><td>November 21, 2026</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="meta-label">Assessor:</td><td>T. Jackson, Sale Fish Marketing and Consulting</td><td class="meta-label">Question Bank:</td><td>v2.1.0</td></tr>
    </table>

    <div style="margin-top:14pt;">
      <div class="kpi-box"><div class="val">1.9</div><div class="lbl">Overall Maturity (CMM 1-5)</div></div>
      <div class="kpi-box"><div class="val">1.9</div><div class="lbl">Risk-Weighted Maturity</div></div>
      <div class="kpi-box"><div class="val">2.9</div><div class="lbl">Industry Baseline (Illustrative)</div></div>
      <div class="kpi-box"><div class="val">20</div><div class="lbl">Risk Items Identified</div></div>
    </div>

    
    <p><strong>Riverside Production Facility</strong> (Meridian Chemical Solutions) — This assessment evaluated <strong>Chemical</strong> OT cybersecurity maturity against <strong>IEC 62443</strong>, cross-referenced to NIST CSF 2.0, using the detailed question set.</p>
    <p>Overall maturity scored <strong>1.9 out of 5.0 (Initial)</strong>, below the illustrative Chemical sector baseline of 2.9. The risk-weighted maturity score is <strong>1.9</strong>.</p>
    <p>The weakest NIST CSF 2.0 function is <strong>Detect</strong> (1.7/5), warranting priority attention. A total of <strong>20 risk item(s)</strong> scored at or below 3.0 and are prioritized in the Top 10 Risks section below.</p>
    <p style="color:var(--text-muted); font-size:13px;">Assessed by: T. Jackson, Sale Fish Marketing and Consulting</p>
  
    <p class="note">N/A responses were excluded from scoring averages for this report. Industry baseline is an illustrative planning estimate, not empirical survey data.</p>

    <div class="page-break"></div>
    <h1>Detailed Summary</h1>
    <h2>Control Family Maturity</h2>
    <img class="chart-img" src="data:image/png;base64," width="600" style="width:600px;" />
    <table class="report-table"><tr><th>ID</th><th>Control Family</th><th>NIST CSF 2.0 Cross-Reference</th><th>CTRL Ref</th><th>Score</th><th>CMM Level</th></tr>
    <tr>
      <td>FR1</td><td>FR1 — Identification &amp; Authentication Control</td><td>PR.AA — Identity Management, Authentication and Access Control</td>
      <td style="font-family:Consolas,monospace; font-size:8.5pt;">CTRL-2.1.1</td>
      <td style="text-align:center;">2.3</td>
      <td>Managed</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>FR2</td><td>FR2 — Use Control</td><td>PR.AA — Identity Management, Authentication and Access Control</td>
      <td style="font-family:Consolas,monospace; font-size:8.5pt;">CTRL-2.1.2</td>
      <td style="text-align:center;">2.0</td>
      <td>Managed</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>FR3</td><td>FR3 — System Integrity</td><td>PR.PS — Platform Security</td>
      <td style="font-family:Consolas,monospace; font-size:8.5pt;">CTRL-2.1.3, CTRL-8.5.1</td>
      <td style="text-align:center;">1.7</td>
      <td>Initial</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>FR4</td><td>FR4 — Data Confidentiality</td><td>PR.DS — Data Security</td>
      <td style="font-family:Consolas,monospace; font-size:8.5pt;">CTRL-3.5.1, CTRL-3.5.2</td>
      <td style="text-align:center;">1.5</td>
      <td>Initial</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>FR5</td><td>FR5 — Restricted Data Flow</td><td>PR.IR — Technology Infrastructure Resilience</td>
      <td style="font-family:Consolas,monospace; font-size:8.5pt;">CTRL-3.2.1, CTRL-3.2.2, CTRL-3.2.3</td>
      <td style="text-align:center;">1.7</td>
      <td>Initial</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>FR6</td><td>FR6 — Timely Response to Events</td><td>DE.AE — Adverse Event Analysis</td>
      <td style="font-family:Consolas,monospace; font-size:8.5pt;">CTRL-6.1.1, CTRL-6.1.2</td>
      <td style="text-align:center;">1.7</td>
      <td>Initial</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>FR7</td><td>FR7 — Resource Availability</td><td>PR.IR — Technology Infrastructure Resilience</td>
      <td style="font-family:Consolas,monospace; font-size:8.5pt;">CTRL-7.4.1, CTRL-7.4.2</td>
      <td style="text-align:center;">2.3</td>
      <td>Managed</td>
    </tr></table>

    <h2>NIST CSF 2.0 Function Rollup</h2>
    <img class="chart-img" src="data:image/png;base64," width="600" style="width:600px;" />
    <table class="report-table"><tr><th>CSF 2.0 Function</th><th>Average Score</th></tr><tr><td>Govern (GV)</td><td style="text-align:center;">—</td></tr><tr><td>Identify (ID)</td><td style="text-align:center;">—</td></tr><tr><td>Protect (PR)</td><td style="text-align:center;">1.9</td></tr><tr><td>Detect (DE)</td><td style="text-align:center;">1.7</td></tr><tr><td>Respond (RS)</td><td style="text-align:center;">—</td></tr><tr><td>Recover (RC)</td><td style="text-align:center;">—</td></tr></table>

    <h2>Maturity vs. Industry Baseline</h2>
    <img class="chart-img" src="data:image/png;base64," width="600" style="width:600px;" />

    <div class="page-break"></div>
    <h1>Top 10 Risks (Prioritized)</h1>
    <p class="note">Priority score = (5 − control score) × control-family criticality weight.</p>
    <table class="report-table"><tr><th>#</th><th>Control Family</th><th>Item</th><th>Score</th><th>Recommended Action</th></tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="text-align:center; font-weight:bold;">1</td><td>FR3 — System Integrity</td><td>Communication integrity is protected on conduits carrying safety-relevant or high-consequence traffic.</td>
      <td style="text-align:center;">1/5</td><td>Implement control-logic change detection tied to your Management of Change process. Verify firmware/software integrity via cryptographic signature before deployment, and protect communication integrity on safety-relevant conduits.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="text-align:center; font-weight:bold;">2</td><td>FR5 — Restricted Data Flow</td><td>Zone/conduit design is re-validated when new device classes are introduced (e.g., IIoT gateways, wireless).</td>
      <td style="text-align:center;">1/5</td><td>Partition OT into risk-based zones (not just topology-convenient ones). Enforce default-deny conduit allow-lists between every zone, and re-validate zone/conduit design whenever new device classes (e.g., IIoT gateways) are introduced.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="text-align:center; font-weight:bold;">3</td><td>FR2 — Use Control</td><td>Use of portable media and mobile code is restricted and controlled at OT boundaries.</td>
      <td style="text-align:center;">1/5</td><td>Enforce least-privilege authorization consistently across zones, scaled to each zone's Security Level. Apply session timeout/concurrent-session limits at jump hosts, and restrict portable media and mobile code at OT boundaries.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="text-align:center; font-weight:bold;">4</td><td>FR6 — Timely Response to Events</td><td>Every zone conduit has an associated detection sensor (passive network monitoring).</td>
      <td style="text-align:center;">1/5</td><td>Ensure every zone conduit has an associated passive detection sensor. Map detection use cases to known adversary tactics/techniques (MITRE ATT&amp;CK for ICS), and rehearse the incident response process via tabletop exercise at least annually.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="text-align:center; font-weight:bold;">5</td><td>FR6 — Timely Response to Events</td><td>Detection use cases are mapped to known adversary tactics/techniques (e.g., MITRE ATT&amp;CK for ICS), not just generic anomaly rules.</td>
      <td style="text-align:center;">1/5</td><td>Ensure every zone conduit has an associated passive detection sensor. Map detection use cases to known adversary tactics/techniques (MITRE ATT&amp;CK for ICS), and rehearse the incident response process via tabletop exercise at least annually.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="text-align:center; font-weight:bold;">6</td><td>FR3 — System Integrity</td><td>Control logic changes are detected and attributed to an authorized change (Management of Change process).</td>
      <td style="text-align:center;">2/5</td><td>Implement control-logic change detection tied to your Management of Change process. Verify firmware/software integrity via cryptographic signature before deployment, and protect communication integrity on safety-relevant conduits.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="text-align:center; font-weight:bold;">7</td><td>FR3 — System Integrity</td><td>Firmware and software integrity is verified via cryptographic signature before deployment.</td>
      <td style="text-align:center;">2/5</td><td>Implement control-logic change detection tied to your Management of Change process. Verify firmware/software integrity via cryptographic signature before deployment, and protect communication integrity on safety-relevant conduits.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="text-align:center; font-weight:bold;">8</td><td>FR5 — Restricted Data Flow</td><td>OT is partitioned into zones based on risk/consequence, not just network topology convenience.</td>
      <td style="text-align:center;">2/5</td><td>Partition OT into risk-based zones (not just topology-convenient ones). Enforce default-deny conduit allow-lists between every zone, and re-validate zone/conduit design whenever new device classes (e.g., IIoT gateways) are introduced.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="text-align:center; font-weight:bold;">9</td><td>FR5 — Restricted Data Flow</td><td>Every conduit between zones has an explicit, documented, default-deny allow-list.</td>
      <td style="text-align:center;">2/5</td><td>Partition OT into risk-based zones (not just topology-convenient ones). Enforce default-deny conduit allow-lists between every zone, and re-validate zone/conduit design whenever new device classes (e.g., IIoT gateways) are introduced.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="text-align:center; font-weight:bold;">10</td><td>FR4 — Data Confidentiality</td><td>Encryption is applied to OT data in transit where the protocol and device support it, and configuration is verified (not just enabled by default).</td>
      <td style="text-align:center;">1/5</td><td>Classify process data, control logic, and network topology information by sensitivity. Verify encryption configuration on OT protocols that support it (don't assume default settings are secure), and document data classification before sending OT data to cloud services.</td>
    </tr></table>

