      <td style="text-align:center;">1</td><td style="text-align:center;">5.20</td>
      <td>Ensure every zone conduit has an associated passive detection sensor. Map detection use cases to known adversary tactics/techniques (MITRE ATT&amp;CK for ICS), and rehearse the incident response process via tabletop exercise at least annually.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>CRITICAL</td><td>FR6</td><td>Detection use cases are mapped to known adversary tactics/techniques (e.g., MITRE ATT&amp;CK for ICS), not just generic anomaly rules.</td>
      <td style="text-align:center;">1</td><td style="text-align:center;">5.20</td>
      <td>Ensure every zone conduit has an associated passive detection sensor. Map detection use cases to known adversary tactics/techniques (MITRE ATT&amp;CK for ICS), and rehearse the incident response process via tabletop exercise at least annually.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>HIGH</td><td>FR3</td><td>Control logic changes are detected and attributed to an authorized change (Management of Change process).</td>
      <td style="text-align:center;">2</td><td style="text-align:center;">4.20</td>
      <td>Implement control-logic change detection tied to your Management of Change process. Verify firmware/software integrity via cryptographic signature before deployment, and protect communication integrity on safety-relevant conduits.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>HIGH</td><td>FR3</td><td>Firmware and software integrity is verified via cryptographic signature before deployment.</td>
      <td style="text-align:center;">2</td><td style="text-align:center;">4.20</td>
      <td>Implement control-logic change detection tied to your Management of Change process. Verify firmware/software integrity via cryptographic signature before deployment, and protect communication integrity on safety-relevant conduits.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>HIGH</td><td>FR5</td><td>OT is partitioned into zones based on risk/consequence, not just network topology convenience.</td>
      <td style="text-align:center;">2</td><td style="text-align:center;">4.20</td>
      <td>Partition OT into risk-based zones (not just topology-convenient ones). Enforce default-deny conduit allow-lists between every zone, and re-validate zone/conduit design whenever new device classes (e.g., IIoT gateways) are introduced.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>HIGH</td><td>FR5</td><td>Every conduit between zones has an explicit, documented, default-deny allow-list.</td>
      <td style="text-align:center;">2</td><td style="text-align:center;">4.20</td>
      <td>Partition OT into risk-based zones (not just topology-convenient ones). Enforce default-deny conduit allow-lists between every zone, and re-validate zone/conduit design whenever new device classes (e.g., IIoT gateways) are introduced.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>CRITICAL</td><td>FR4</td><td>Encryption is applied to OT data in transit where the protocol and device support it, and configuration is verified (not just enabled by default).</td>
      <td style="text-align:center;">1</td><td style="text-align:center;">4.00</td>
      <td>Classify process data, control logic, and network topology information by sensitivity. Verify encryption configuration on OT protocols that support it (don't assume default settings are secure), and document data classification before sending OT data to cloud services.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>HIGH</td><td>FR2</td><td>Least-privilege authorization is enforced and enforced consistently across zones of differing Security Levels.</td>
      <td style="text-align:center;">2</td><td style="text-align:center;">3.90</td>
      <td>Enforce least-privilege authorization consistently across zones, scaled to each zone's Security Level. Apply session timeout/concurrent-session limits at jump hosts, and restrict portable media and mobile code at OT boundaries.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>HIGH</td><td>FR7</td><td>Verified backups (golden images) exist for high-criticality assets and are actually restore-tested.</td>
      <td style="text-align:center;">2</td><td style="text-align:center;">3.90</td>
      <td>Maintain verified, restore-tested golden-image backups for high-criticality assets. Define RTO/RPO per criticality tier with operations/safety approval, and design networks for graceful degradation rather than cascading failure.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>HIGH</td><td>FR7</td><td>Recovery Time/Point Objectives are defined per criticality tier and approved by operations/safety.</td>
      <td style="text-align:center;">2</td><td style="text-align:center;">3.90</td>
      <td>Maintain verified, restore-tested golden-image backups for high-criticality assets. Define RTO/RPO per criticality tier with operations/safety approval, and design networks for graceful degradation rather than cascading failure.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>HIGH</td><td>FR1</td><td>Software processes and devices are authenticated before being permitted to communicate on OT networks.</td>
      <td style="text-align:center;">2</td><td style="text-align:center;">3.60</td>
      <td>Uniquely identify and authenticate all human users, software processes, and devices before granting OT access. Implement certificate-based device identity where supported (e.g., OPC-UA) and scale authentication strength to each zone's Security Level Target.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>HIGH</td><td>FR1</td><td>Authentication credentials are managed with a defined lifecycle appropriate to each Security Level (SL-T).</td>
      <td style="text-align:center;">2</td><td style="text-align:center;">3.60</td>
      <td>Uniquely identify and authenticate all human users, software processes, and devices before granting OT access. Implement certificate-based device identity where supported (e.g., OPC-UA) and scale authentication strength to each zone's Security Level Target.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>HIGH</td><td>FR4</td><td>Process data, control logic, and network topology information are classified and handled per their sensitivity.</td>
      <td style="text-align:center;">2</td><td style="text-align:center;">3.00</td>
      <td>Classify process data, control logic, and network topology information by sensitivity. Verify encryption configuration on OT protocols that support it (don't assume default settings are secure), and document data classification before sending OT data to cloud services.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>MEDIUM</td><td>FR2</td><td>Session controls (timeout, concurrent session limits) are applied to privileged OT sessions.</td>
      <td style="text-align:center;">3</td><td style="text-align:center;">2.60</td>
      <td>Enforce least-privilege authorization consistently across zones, scaled to each zone's Security Level. Apply session timeout/concurrent-session limits at jump hosts, and restrict portable media and mobile code at OT boundaries.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>MEDIUM</td><td>FR6</td><td>A documented, rehearsed incident response process exists with safety-first containment decision criteria.</td>
      <td style="text-align:center;">3</td><td style="text-align:center;">2.60</td>
      <td>Ensure every zone conduit has an associated passive detection sensor. Map detection use cases to known adversary tactics/techniques (MITRE ATT&amp;CK for ICS), and rehearse the incident response process via tabletop exercise at least annually.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>MEDIUM</td><td>FR7</td><td>Network and system design accounts for graceful degradation rather than cascading failure under resource exhaustion (e.g., DoS, broadcast storm).</td>
      <td style="text-align:center;">3</td><td style="text-align:center;">2.60</td>
      <td>Maintain verified, restore-tested golden-image backups for high-criticality assets. Define RTO/RPO per criticality tier with operations/safety approval, and design networks for graceful degradation rather than cascading failure.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>MEDIUM</td><td>FR1</td><td>All human users are uniquely identified and authenticated before being granted access to OT systems.</td>
      <td style="text-align:center;">3</td><td style="text-align:center;">2.40</td>
      <td>Uniquely identify and authenticate all human users, software processes, and devices before granting OT access. Implement certificate-based device identity where supported (e.g., OPC-UA) and scale authentication strength to each zone's Security Level Target.</td>
    </tr></table>

    <div class="page-break"></div>
    <h1>Full Assessment Detail (Appendix)</h1>
    <p class="note">Every question and response, including items that scored above the risk threshold.</p>
    <table class="report-table"><tr><th>Family</th><th>Question</th><th>Score</th><th>Notes</th></tr><tr><td>FR1</td><td>All human users are uniquely identified and authenticated before being granted access to OT systems.</td><td style="text-align:center;">3</td><td>Operators share two HMI logins per shift; engineering accounts are individual. Domain accounts for EWS only.</td></tr><tr><td>FR1</td><td>Software processes and devices are authenticated before being permitted to communicate on OT networks.</td><td style="text-align:center;">2</td><td>Operators share two HMI logins per shift; engineering accounts are individual. Domain accounts for EWS only.</td></tr><tr><td>FR1</td><td>Authentication credentials are managed with a defined lifecycle appropriate to each Security Level (SL-T).</td><td style="text-align:center;">2</td><td>Operators share two HMI logins per shift; engineering accounts are individual. Domain accounts for EWS only.</td></tr><tr><td>FR2</td><td>Least-privilege authorization is enforced and enforced consistently across zones of differing Security Levels.</td><td style="text-align:center;">2</td><td>USB ports disabled on HMIs, but the two engineering laptops are unrestricted.</td></tr><tr><td>FR2</td><td>Session controls (timeout, concurrent session limits) are applied to privileged OT sessions.</td><td style="text-align:center;">3</td><td>USB ports disabled on HMIs, but the two engineering laptops are unrestricted.</td></tr><tr><td>FR2</td><td>Use of portable media and mobile code is restricted and controlled at OT boundaries.</td><td style="text-align:center;">1</td><td>USB ports disabled on HMIs, but the two engineering laptops are unrestricted.</td></tr><tr><td>FR3</td><td>Control logic changes are detected and attributed to an authorized change (Management of Change process).</td><td style="text-align:center;">2</td><td>No logic-change detection. Firmware is downloaded from vendor portals without signature checks.</td></tr><tr><td>FR3</td><td>Firmware and software integrity is verified via cryptographic signature before deployment.</td><td style="text-align:center;">2</td><td>No logic-change detection. Firmware is downloaded from vendor portals without signature checks.</td></tr><tr><td>FR3</td><td>Communication integrity is protected on conduits carrying safety-relevant or high-consequence traffic.</td><td style="text-align:center;">1</td><td>No logic-change detection. Firmware is downloaded from vendor portals without signature checks.</td></tr><tr><td>FR4</td><td>Process data, control logic, and network topology information are classified and handled per their sensitivity.</td><td style="text-align:center;">2</td><td>No cloud services in use (FR4 Q3 marked N/A). Historian shares process data with corporate by file transfer.</td></tr><tr><td>FR4</td><td>Encryption is applied to OT data in transit where the protocol and device support it, and configuration is verified (not just enabled by default).</td><td style="text-align:center;">1</td><td>No cloud services in use (FR4 Q3 marked N/A). Historian shares process data with corporate by file transfer.</td></tr><tr><td>FR4</td><td>Data sent to cloud-hosted OT services (historians, analytics) has a documented data classification and shared-responsibility review.</td><td style="text-align:center;">N/A</td><td>No cloud services in use (FR4 Q3 marked N/A). Historian shares process data with corporate by file transfer.</td></tr><tr><td>FR5</td><td>OT is partitioned into zones based on risk/consequence, not just network topology convenience.</td><td style="text-align:center;">2</td><td>Flat control network: Lines 1–3, SIS engineering and historian share one VLAN. Firewall to corporate is 'any-any' for 10.20.0.0/16.</td></tr><tr><td>FR5</td><td>Every conduit between zones has an explicit, documented, default-deny allow-list.</td><td style="text-align:center;">2</td><td>Flat control network: Lines 1–3, SIS engineering and historian share one VLAN. Firewall to corporate is 'any-any' for 10.20.0.0/16.</td></tr><tr><td>FR5</td><td>Zone/conduit design is re-validated when new device classes are introduced (e.g., IIoT gateways, wireless).</td><td style="text-align:center;">1</td><td>Flat control network: Lines 1–3, SIS engineering and historian share one VLAN. Firewall to corporate is 'any-any' for 10.20.0.0/16.</td></tr><tr><td>FR6</td><td>Every zone conduit has an associated detection sensor (passive network monitoring).</td><td style="text-align:center;">1</td><td>No OT network monitoring. IR plan exists (corporate IT plan) and was used in the September tabletop.</td></tr><tr><td>FR6</td><td>Detection use cases are mapped to known adversary tactics/techniques (e.g., MITRE ATT&amp;CK for ICS), not just generic anomaly rules.</td><td style="text-align:center;">1</td><td>No OT network monitoring. IR plan exists (corporate IT plan) and was used in the September tabletop.</td></tr><tr><td>FR6</td><td>A documented, rehearsed incident response process exists with safety-first containment decision criteria.</td><td style="text-align:center;">3</td><td>No OT network monitoring. IR plan exists (corporate IT plan) and was used in the September tabletop.</td></tr><tr><td>FR7</td><td>Verified backups (golden images) exist for high-criticality assets and are actually restore-tested.</td><td style="text-align:center;">2</td><td>PLC backups taken ad hoc by the controls engineer; last full restore test unknown.</td></tr><tr><td>FR7</td><td>Recovery Time/Point Objectives are defined per criticality tier and approved by operations/safety.</td><td style="text-align:center;">2</td><td>PLC backups taken ad hoc by the controls engineer; last full restore test unknown.</td></tr><tr><td>FR7</td><td>Network and system design accounts for graceful degradation rather than cascading failure under resource exhaustion (e.g., DoS, broadcast storm).</td><td style="text-align:center;">3</td><td>PLC backups taken ad hoc by the controls engineer; last full restore test unknown.</td></tr></table>

    
